        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ఎందుకంటే",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ఎందుకంటే (endukaṇṭē) — \"because\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Look at it: it is ఎందుకు (enduku) with అంటే glued on — \"if you ask why\". Telugu answers the question by repeating it and then continuing, which is the gluing habit of your very first lesson doing real work."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "నేను వెళ్తాను, ఎందుకంటే (endukaṇṭē) పని ఉంది — \"I'll go, because there is work.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the reason clause comes second",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Telugu can put the reason first, as పని ఉంది కాబట్టి నేను వెళ్తాను. With ఎందుకంటే (endukaṇṭē) it goes second, after the thing being explained, exactly where English puts it. Use ఎందుకంటే (endukaṇṭē) while that ordering is still the one you think in."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A reason, attached to the thing it explains."
            }
          ]
        },
